# Vercel Web Analytics Installation

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics for this static HTML project.

## Changes Made

### Modified Files
- `launchpad/index.html` - Added Vercel Web Analytics tracking scripts

## Implementation Details

Following the official Vercel documentation (https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart), I added the Web Analytics scripts to the HTML file.

### What was added:
Added the following code immediately after the existing Speed Insights script (before the closing `</head>` tag):

```html
<script>
  window.va = window.va || function () { (window.vaq = window.vaq || []).push(arguments); };
</script>
<script defer src="/_vercel/insights/script.js"></script>
```

This is the recommended approach for static HTML sites according to Vercel's documentation.

## Framework
This is a static HTML site hosted on Vercel. The project structure shows:
- Static HTML files
- Serverless API functions in `/api` directory
- Vercel configuration in `vercel.json`

## Package Management
Since this is a static HTML site without a package.json or Node.js dependencies, there was no need to:
- Install npm packages
- Run build commands
- Update lockfiles

The Web Analytics will be loaded directly from Vercel's CDN at `/_vercel/insights/script.js` after deployment.

## Next Steps
After deploying to Vercel:
1. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard for this project
2. Navigate to the Analytics section and click "Enable"
3. After the next deployment, analytics data will start being collected
4. Verify installation by checking the browser Network tab for requests to `/_vercel/insights/*`

## Notes
- The project already had Vercel Speed Insights installed, showing familiarity with Vercel's analytics tools
- Web Analytics and Speed Insights work together to provide comprehensive analytics coverage
- No build process or testing was required as this is a static HTML site
- The scripts are deferred and loaded asynchronously to avoid blocking page load
